{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/pulls/403","id":3864240198,"node_id":"PR_kwDOAH7Cv87mU6BG","html_url":"https://github.com/mahmoud/boltons/pull/403","diff_url":"https://github.com/mahmoud/boltons/pull/403.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/mahmoud/boltons/pull/403.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/issues/403","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/pulls/403/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/pulls/403/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/issues/403/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mahmoud/boltons/statuses/bfeca9dd304745e0faf62e78351f401bbdd7b05d","number":403,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Fix bytes2human rollover at exact powers of 1024","user":{"login":"patchwright","id":292882882,"node_id":"U_kgDOEXUJwg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/292882882?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright","html_url":"https://github.com/patchwright","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/patchwright/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## What\n\n`bytes2human` doesn't roll over to the next unit at exact powers of 1024:\n\n```python\n>>> from boltons.strutils import bytes2human\n>>> bytes2human(1024)        # expected '1K'\n'1024B'\n>>> bytes2human(1024 ** 2)   # expected '1M'\n'1024K'\n>>> bytes2human(1024 ** 3)   # expected '1G'\n'1024M'\n```\n\nSub-boundary values are fine (`bytes2human(1023) == '1023B'`, `bytes2human(2048) == '2K'`); only the exact boundaries are wrong.\n\n## Why\n\nThe unit-selection loop compares the value against each boundary with `<=`:\n\n```python\nfor (size, symbol), (next_size, next_symbol) in _SIZE_RANGES:\n    if abs_bytes <= next_size:\n        break\n```\n\n`_SIZE_RANGES` pairs each unit with the next one's threshold (`((1, 'B'), (1024, 'K'))`, …). When `abs_bytes` equals a threshold exactly (e.g. `1024`), `<=` breaks on the *smaller* unit, so the value renders as the previous unit × 1024. Using `<` lets exact boundaries advance to the next unit.\n\n## Change\n\n- `<=` → `<` in the boundary comparison (one character).\n- Added a doctest (`bytes2human(1024) -> '1K'`) and a regression test in `tests/test_strutils.py` covering boundaries, sub-boundaries, mid-range, negatives and `ndigits`. The test was verified to fail on the current code and pass with the fix.\n\nNo behavior change for any non-boundary value. `pytest tests/` and `pytest --doctest-modules boltons/strutils.py` both pass. Happy to add a CHANGELOG entry in whatever form you prefer.\n","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-06-14T23:22:26Z","updated_at":"2026-06-17T20:55:45Z","closed_at":"2026-06-17T20:55:45Z","merged_at":"2026-06-17T20:55:45Z","merge_commit_sha":"766b5547392fe8d54c1e000098499dc556bb5cb7","assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"head":{"label":"patchwright:fix/bytes2human-power-of-1024-boundary","ref":"fix/bytes2human-power-of-1024-boundary","repo":{"id":1269607449,"node_id":"R_kgDOS6ysGQ","name":"boltons","full_name":"patchwright/boltons","owner":{"login":"patchwright","id":292882882,"node_id":"U_kgDOEXUJwg","avata